			<div class="content" id="post-content-3825698">
									

<p>Though not my kind of read, the concept itself isn't bad. But I'm with Shaggydredlocks on this.</p>
<p>First, only the last redaction is warranted. The rest isn't helping with the sense of a paranoid Foundation. Instead, they are creating the sense of a Foundation that wants to seed paranoia among its own ranks, because it creates too many questions and, at the end, it is simply technical details easy to guess from what is already given.</p>
<p>Also, the conclusion is forced and the new SCProcedures seem to be for shock value. The Foundation have no reason whatsoever to conclude time travel, there is still an infinite array of other possibilities, ranging from spies to other dimensions. IF the time travel theory had any evidence behind it, it would still be unlikely for they to fear a paradox. Several anomalies deal with time in some fashion, and each is so unique that you can't reach standard conclusions about consequences that way. What, after all, would a paradox cause?</p>
<p>Even if the Time Travel was confirmed, it isn't very like them to simply let an anomaly have it its way. They would, instead, keep the first procedures and set more thorough surveillance over the -Bs. You could, instead, set the first SCProcedures to include freeing the children after they are confirmed non-anomalous, and then the second one is to keep them contained indefinitely. Or you can simply drop the change in containment altogether.</p>

							</div>

								
					
